1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:35,415
Welcome to the technical briefing on Prism-A-N-N version three: a co-designed enterprise retrieval prototype combining hierarchical SpiceDB permissions with filtered H-N-S-W search. Across one hundred sixty nine thousand eight hundred ten confirmatory queries on two public corpora plus three reviewer audits, Prism-A-N-N cuts broad-principal pre-filtering by up to two hundred seventy eight times, accelerates PostgreSQL folder revocation with zero vector writes, and observes zero leaks across the tested workloads.

2
00:00:36,215 --> 00:01:21,880
Why do enterprise retrieval systems struggle today? When hierarchical Zanzibar permissions meet H-N-S-W vector indexes, three severe bottlenecks emerge. First, streaming thousands of leaf document IDs from PostgreSQL-backed SpiceDB stalls broad queries for ninety eight milliseconds median before vector search even starts. Second, when a user can only view zero point one percent of a corpus, standard FAISS H-N-S-W collapses to five point seven five percent recall, while unbounded graph walks spike up to one hundred forty seven milliseconds. Third, storing permissions inline on vector rows causes massive write amplification: revoking one folder across one hundred fifty three thousand chunks stalls pgvector for nearly three full minutes.

3
00:01:22,680 --> 00:02:02,199
Prism-A-N-N addresses all three bottlenecks by co-designing the authorization graph, the vector index, and the hardware scanner. In Layer One, Upward Boundary Pruning stamps structural lineages at ingest and halts SpiceDB traversal above leaf documents, splitting only around explicit deny walls—extended with atomic revisioned lineage graphs when hierarchy edges change post-index. In Layer Two, Geometric Express Bridges add eight diverse semantic shortcuts per node across folders, parent containers, and Meaning Magnets for one point nine one percent extra graph memory. In Layer Three, Z-ordered Roaring bitmaps feed contiguous memory spans straight into a native C-plus-plus ARM NEON kernel with a multi-stage Local-Stall Guard.

4
00:02:02,999 --> 00:02:38,755
Our first confirmatory experiment tests live SpiceDB version one point fifty seven in both memory and durable PostgreSQL seventeen modes across twelve hundred principals with up to twenty deny walls. Upward Boundary Pruning matches SpiceDB with zero mismatches on static-hierarchy workloads and obeys Theorem One across all twelve hundred principals. For broad principals accessing over one thousand documents, pre-filter compilation drops from ninety eight milliseconds on PostgreSQL SpiceDB down to zero point three five milliseconds on a local mirror, and three point four nine milliseconds when calling live PostgreSQL SpiceDB gRPC.

5
00:02:39,555 --> 00:03:21,382
Next, we benchmarked live native engines across five hundred twenty two thousand BEIR Quora passages and two hundred fifty thousand Wikipedia passages. Graph-only geometric bridges beat FAISS M equals sixteen by up to sixty one recall points, and beat compiled upstream C-plus-plus ACORN-1 and ACORN-gamma across tested selectivities on both corpora. When the ARM NEON Mode Three scanner engages at one percent selectivity and below, Prism-A-N-N empirically reaches one hundred percent recall—mathematically exact under FP32 mode or small candidate counts—in zero point zero five to zero point eleven milliseconds kernel time, or one point six to two milliseconds end-to-end with live PostgreSQL SpiceDB RPC.

6
00:03:22,182 --> 00:03:57,741
In real enterprise deployments, the hardest failure mode occurs when a user's permitted documents sit far from the query in embedding space. Under negative Global-Local Selectivity correlation, FAISS collapses to five percent recall, and hnswlib takes up to forty five milliseconds. Prism-A-N-N's multi-stage Local-Stall Guard detects stalled queries and escalates them to Mode Three, restoring ninety seven point two percent recall on Quora and ninety six point four percent on Wikipedia in zero point one seven to zero point three five milliseconds kernel latency.

7
00:03:58,541 --> 00:04:33,255
Compliance teams care deeply about revocation speed: when an employee leaves a project or an ethical wall goes up, access must vanish cleanly. Updating an inline permission column across one hundred fifty three thousand chunks in PostgreSQL pgvector triggers heavy heap rewrites and index maintenance, taking one hundred seventy nine seconds median. With Prism-A-N-N, revoking the exact same folder deletes a single edge in persistent PostgreSQL SpiceDB in sixty seven milliseconds with zero vector-store writes, bounded causally by atomic ZedToken and lineage-revision snapshots.

8
00:04:34,055 --> 00:05:03,429
To verify our gains are not an artifact of lucky entry points, we ran a four-way ablation on the exact same five hundred twenty two thousand node FAISS base graph. Adding four authorized entry portals to plain FAISS moves recall by less than one point. Conversely, removing portals completely from our geometric bridge graph retains over one hundred one percent of the recall lift across all sub-one-percent selectivities, showing that Vamana-diverse intra-container bridges drive the connectivity gain.

9
00:05:04,229 --> 00:05:39,231
Scientific credibility requires reporting every limitation and falsified prediction transparently. Across our reviewer audits covering two hundred seven thousand total records, three strict pre-registered latency cutoffs—PR7, PR8, and ultra-low zero-point-zero-one-percent live-RPC PR10—were falsified as predicted thresholds and reported honestly alongside positive results. We separate our one point nine one percent graph-only memory cost from the twenty four point eight percent footprint when retaining an auxiliary eight-bit buffer, and we measure exact FP32 Mode Three beating in-timer SQ8 below zero point one percent selectivity.

10
00:05:40,031 --> 00:06:09,660
To summarize what remains before conference submission: while zero leaks were observed across one thousand independent dynamic multi-parent DAGs and eight-thread live opaque-ZedToken SpiceDB runs, Prism-A-N-N has not yet been benchmarked against upstream binaries of RACORN, EMA, Curator, or Veda or validated at billion-vector scale. All reproducible proofs, pre-registered protocols, raw logs, and falsified checks are open in the repository for independent reproduction. Thank you for watching.
